LOCAL COUNTER-EVIDENCE (tick bunker-2026-09-21-03-48-44, HEAD 19936bc): the exact cell PASSES in the working tree. Built the CLI (`go build -o /tmp/bunker-check ./cmd/bunker`) and ran the cell's command verbatim: `env -u BUNKER_SESSION_TARGET /tmp/bunker-check spawn --agent-id should-refuse` → stdout 'bunker: no target bound: pass --server/--agent or set BUNKER_SESSION_TARGET (mutating commands never fall back to the shared 'bunker use' default ...)' exit 1. So the GAP-093 refusal contract itself is intact and the message still matches the grep. The CI failure is therefore a SUITE/ENVIRONMENT effect, not a product regression: candidates to check in regression-tests.sh — (a) the nested E2E battery runs the cell as a non-root/unprivileged user where the refusal path exits before printing, (b) the nested suite inherits BUNKER_SESSION_TARGET from an earlier section so `env -u` is not actually clearing it, or (c) the child binary resolved on PATH inside the nested run is the pre-a4c1623 build cached at /tmp/bunker-cli-build-cache-uid1000 (see INT-CI-023). Also note the E2E battery step is currently UNREACHABLE on 10907e3 because the Regression suite step fails first (INT-CI-030) — fixing INT-CI-030 is a precondition for re-observing this cell.

SECOND MEASUREMENT (run 35561265258 on bf4caf1, where the Regression suite step finally passed FAIL: 0 and the battery step therefore ran): the battery's NESTED regression run reproduces FAIL: 3 and the visible cell is again '✗ unbound spawn refused again when re-checked for its exit code', while the SAME suite standalone in the sibling job is FAIL: 0 and the cell passes locally on a built HEAD binary. That contrast (standalone green, nested red) sharpens the hypothesis list to environment inheritance rather than product behaviour — most likely the nested run resolves a different `bunker` binary or inherits a binding/HOME the standalone job does not (see INT-CI-023's shared per-uid CLI build cache at /tmp/bunker-cli-build-cache-uid1000; the battery exports its own BUNKER_HOME + target). Next action: dump `command -v bunker` + its build hash and `env | grep BUNKER` from INSIDE the nested run's failing cell before changing any product code.
